# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Database — a build artifact, reproduced by replaying seed/ + data/raw/.
# See docs/design-decisions.md "The database is not committed".
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# Data: ignore everything except the raw source snapshots, which ARE the evidence.
data/*
!data/raw/
!data/raw/**
!data/.gitkeep

# Census reference data is the exception to the rule above: 3.8 MB of national
# lookup tables that are not evidence of any project claim, are byte-identical for
# everyone, and are re-downloadable from a stable URL. `tracker ingest geo` names
# the URLs when they are absent. See docs/design-decisions.md "Deriving county
# and coordinates".
data/raw/census/
!data/exports/
data/exports/*
!data/exports/.gitkeep

# Ingest run artifacts
.cache/
runs/
rejects*.jsonl

# Operator-private seeds (never share)
seed/private-*.json

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.venv/
venv/

# Tooling
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml

# Claude Code: instructions are COMMITTED, machine state is not.
#
# This whole directory used to be ignored, and that quietly cost us the thing it
# was protecting. Agents now run on the production host, in worktrees of this
# repo — and a worktree contains exactly what is committed, so an ignored
# `.claude/` meant the one real deploy procedure we had lived on a single laptop
# and no agent had ever seen it. Two machines were following different rules
# because only one of them had been given any.
#
# So the split is by kind rather than by directory. `commands/` and `skills/` are
# instructions: they describe this project and belong to every checkout of it.
# The rest is per-machine state — which server a preview starts, which
# permissions one person granted, a session lock — and would only ever conflict.
.claude/*
!.claude/commands/
!.claude/skills/
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/launch.json
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
.claude/worktrees/

# Multica injects its own platform skill and a task-context file into each agent
# workdir as a sidecar. They belong to the run, not to the repo, and without
# these lines every agent would see them as untracked changes to commit.
.claude/skills/multica-platform/
.multica/

# The marker that says a checkout IS production: it serves the console and owns
# the authoritative database. Untracked on purpose — exactly one checkout in the
# world may carry it, so a committed copy would tell every clone it was the
# writer. See CLAUDE.md §2.
.production

# Working documents and client deliverables. Only *technical* docs are committed —
# the ones a person reading this codebase needs. See CLAUDE.md §6 for the rule.
#
# Plans, reviews and feedback are dated snapshots of a decision in progress: they
# go stale the moment the code lands, and a reader who finds one cannot tell
# whether it describes the system or an argument somebody had about it. The
# Chinese-language documents are client deliverables, not repo documentation.
docs/plan-*.md
docs/*-plan.md
docs/review-*.md
docs/*-review-*.md
docs/feedback-*.md
docs/*.zh-CN.md

# Deployment tooling and the session log: host-specific by nature. `deploy/` names
# the production host in its launchd plist filenames and its runbook is procedure
# for that one machine; HANDOFF.md is a dated working log. Both stay on disk and
# out of the repo. CLAUDE.md is committed but names no host — see its §6.
deploy/
HANDOFF.md

# Only Markdown is documentation. Decks, PDFs and spreadsheets under docs/ are
# client deliverables by nature — same rule as the Chinese-language documents.
docs/*.pdf
docs/*.pptx
docs/*.docx
docs/*.xlsx

# Same rule outside docs/: review decks and PDFs staged for a client walkthrough.
# Flagged untracked by five straight housekeeping runs (2026-08-21 through
# 2026-08-25) with no gitignore line of its own, so a `git add -A` would have
# swept eight binary files into a public repo the moment someone ran one.
work/
